WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.773
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:05.043 --> 00:06.845
Case one.

3
00:07.545 --> 00:10.949
The cash theft in Chiba District 17.

4
00:11.616 --> 00:14.819
Mr. Kiuchi was eating hamburger steak

5
00:14.886 --> 00:17.956
while the bag of campaign money stayed in his car trunk.

6
00:18.022 --> 00:21.626
At some point, his car key was found on the floor.

7
00:21.693 --> 00:23.828
He remembered that later.

8
00:23.895 --> 00:25.763
But there were no security cameras

9
00:25.830 --> 00:27.732
inside the restaurant or in the parking lot.

10
00:27.799 --> 00:29.234
No clues at all.

11
00:29.300 --> 00:31.002
Case two.

12
00:31.069 --> 00:34.205
The Ponz Next office cash theft.

13
00:34.272 --> 00:35.974
We don't know the details.

14
00:36.040 --> 00:38.510
The president and Mr. Ienaga

15
00:38.576 --> 00:40.411
have both kept silent.

16
00:40.478 --> 00:44.115
Both cases involve Miyabi Kobayakawa,

17
00:44.182 --> 00:47.085
but I can't believe Ms. Miyabi would steal.

18
00:47.152 --> 00:50.855
The suspicious one is her secretary, Yayoi Kisaragi.

19
00:50.922 --> 00:52.123
Thank you for waiting.

20
00:52.190 --> 00:55.493
One Dolce latte and an iced coffee. That's 1,250 yen.

21
00:55.560 --> 00:57.629
I only have 500 yen in small change.

22
00:58.196 --> 00:59.531
I'll pay you back later.

23
00:59.597 --> 01:01.366
Here's your 250 yen change.

24
01:12.043 --> 01:15.914
What kind of person is she, really?

25
01:16.915 --> 01:19.517
Yayoi, do you have siblings? Are you an only child?

26
01:22.320 --> 01:23.755
Many, both older and younger.

27
01:23.821 --> 01:25.790
Do you have like ten siblings?

28
01:25.857 --> 01:27.125
That's a lot.

29
01:28.393 --> 01:29.561
Where are you from?

30
01:31.129 --> 01:32.997
A place full of nature.

31
01:33.064 --> 01:33.932
Countryside?

32
01:33.998 --> 01:35.300
Don't look down on the countryside.

33
01:35.366 --> 01:36.467
Chiba is countryside too.

34
01:36.534 --> 01:37.502
Different level.

35
01:37.569 --> 01:40.638
Excuse me! May I have a moment?

36
01:40.705 --> 01:43.575
We're asking people their annual income.

37
01:43.641 --> 01:45.376
-Oh, I know your show. -Really?

38
01:45.443 --> 01:47.011
Then let's film you too, miss.

39
01:47.078 --> 01:49.480
Straight to the point! What's your annual income?

40
01:49.547 --> 01:51.316
About 20 million yen.

41
01:51.382 --> 01:53.685
What? What's your job?

42
01:53.751 --> 01:55.353
I'm a Diet member.

43
01:55.420 --> 01:56.854
So I'm paid annually.

44
01:56.921 --> 01:58.389
ANNUAL PAY 20 MILLION YEN

45
01:58.456 --> 02:02.160
And I also have a research, PR, and lodging allowance

46
02:02.227 --> 02:03.595
of one million yen every month.

47
02:03.661 --> 02:07.565
I have a free JR pass. Airline tickets are provided too.

48
02:07.632 --> 02:08.766
Hey, this isn't good.

49
02:08.833 --> 02:10.468
I mean, anyone can look it up.

50
02:11.035 --> 02:15.707
Also, since I joined the Kokuji Party, I get party grants too.

51
02:15.773 --> 02:17.275
Ten million a year.

52
02:17.342 --> 02:18.243
TEN MILLION A YEAR

53
02:18.309 --> 02:20.979
Add it all up, about 50 million a year?

54
02:21.045 --> 02:21.879
FIFTY MILLION!

55
02:21.946 --> 02:23.448
Fifty million?!

56
02:24.015 --> 02:27.018
Straight to the point! What's your annual income?

57
02:27.085 --> 02:28.620
About 20 million yen.

58
02:28.686 --> 02:30.688
What? What's your job?

59
02:31.256 --> 02:32.590
I'm a Diet member.

60
02:32.657 --> 02:34.158
So I'm paid annually.

61
02:34.726 --> 02:38.363
And I also have a research, PR, and lodging allowance…

62
02:38.429 --> 02:42.133
Seems the economy is just peachy these days.

63
02:42.200 --> 02:44.235
Airline tickets are provided too.

64
02:44.769 --> 02:46.070
Hey, this isn't good.

65
02:46.137 --> 02:48.172
I mean, anyone can look it up.

66
02:48.239 --> 02:49.574
PERSONAL SECRETARY

67
02:49.641 --> 02:51.909
Since I joined the Kokuji Party…

68
02:51.976 --> 02:55.046
A Diet member's income has three main parts.

69
02:55.113 --> 02:57.815
Salary, party grants, and donations.

70
02:58.383 --> 03:00.685
Donations simply mean contributions.

71
03:01.853 --> 03:06.224
The salary goes into the member's personal account.

72
03:07.492 --> 03:11.129
But the second and third must not be paid into a personal account.

73
03:11.195 --> 03:13.364
Party grants can only be received

74
03:13.431 --> 03:18.536
through the political party branch's account.

75
03:18.603 --> 03:22.607
For House members, there can only be one party branch for each district.

76
03:23.341 --> 03:28.313
Hayate already made a branch for "Chiba 17."

77
03:28.379 --> 03:31.316
So we can't have one?

78
03:31.382 --> 03:35.119
No, no. We can make "Kokuji Party, Chiba Prefecture, District 17 Branch."

79
03:35.186 --> 03:36.654
Change the name a little.

80
03:37.322 --> 03:39.057
-Really? -Huh?

81
03:39.123 --> 03:41.125
CHIBA 17 BRANCH CHIBA DISTRICT 17 BRANCH

82
03:41.192 --> 03:43.528
The Secretary General already gave us permission to set it up.

83
03:43.594 --> 03:47.665
Please open an account at the bank on the 4th floor, at Risuno Bank.

84
03:47.732 --> 03:49.000
RISUNO BANK

85
03:49.067 --> 03:50.234
It's done.

86
03:51.102 --> 03:53.037
Risuno Bank, House of Representatives branch.

87
03:53.104 --> 03:56.140
"Kokuji Party, Chiba Prefecture, District 17 Branch!"

88
03:56.207 --> 03:57.809
Slightly different name!

89
03:58.509 --> 04:00.478
A tough game of find-the-difference.

90
04:00.545 --> 04:01.612
Ta-da!

91
04:01.679 --> 04:03.147
Our party grant,

92
04:03.214 --> 04:05.049
There's 2.5 million yen!

93
04:06.818 --> 04:10.488
My restaurant's yearly income, earned in an instant…

94
04:11.289 --> 04:14.959
Independents don't get this money, but party candidates do.

95
04:15.026 --> 04:16.361
Isn't that unfair?

96
04:16.427 --> 04:17.829
I agree!

97
04:17.895 --> 04:21.966
It's unfair that only established parties can get this grant!

98
04:22.033 --> 04:25.436
How much grant you get is decided by the number of party members.

99
04:25.503 --> 04:26.604
How can we steal it?

100
04:26.671 --> 04:29.173
If you gather five members and make your own party,

101
04:29.240 --> 04:30.241
you can have it all!

102
04:30.308 --> 04:33.611
How the money is used is up to the party. The party leader could take all of it.

103
04:33.678 --> 04:37.281
How sloppy! Why do party grants exist in the first place?

104
04:37.348 --> 04:41.085
Good question. The story goes back 30 years.

105
04:41.819 --> 04:43.020
Tell me.

106
04:43.087 --> 04:45.556
Nipponia, 30 years ago.

107
04:45.623 --> 04:50.161
It was discovered that companies were bribing politicians with lots of money.

108
04:50.228 --> 04:53.531
The people got seriously angry.

109
04:53.598 --> 04:56.868
"Enough! No more money in politics!"

110
04:56.934 --> 05:00.004
"How about we ban companies from making donations?"

111
05:00.071 --> 05:00.838
"What?"

112
05:00.905 --> 05:03.341
"In exchange, please let us collect

113
05:03.408 --> 05:06.177
250 yen from each citizen."

114
05:06.244 --> 05:08.746
"We'll divide it among the parties."

115
05:08.813 --> 05:11.516
"You can have clean politics for the price of one cup of coffee!"

116
05:11.582 --> 05:13.551
"Wow! We did it!"

117
05:13.618 --> 05:16.454
That's how the party grant system began.

118
05:16.521 --> 05:19.290
But the politicians made a loophole.

119
05:19.357 --> 05:24.095
Individual members may not receive corporate donations.

120
05:24.162 --> 05:28.232
But party branches may do it.

121
05:28.299 --> 05:31.235
So party branches multiplied, one for each member.

122
05:31.302 --> 05:35.373
Corporate donations only changed their destinations, and here we are today.

123
05:35.440 --> 05:37.408
"I get money twice! Lucky!"

124
05:37.475 --> 05:40.845
Politicians don't want to reform the laws if it will hurt them.

125
05:40.912 --> 05:42.513
They always make loopholes.

126
05:42.580 --> 05:43.681
You know what?

127
05:44.382 --> 05:48.519
I think citizens' income and politicians' pay should be linked.

128
05:48.586 --> 05:51.622
If the average income falls, politicians' pay falls also.

129
05:51.689 --> 05:53.991
Then they would work desperately to raise wages.

130
05:54.058 --> 05:56.694
Well, whatever.

131
05:56.761 --> 05:57.929
Don't "whatever" me.

132
05:57.995 --> 06:00.097
I understand what you said.

133
06:00.164 --> 06:03.100
Party grants are the people's precious 250 yen.

134
06:03.167 --> 06:06.103
As a great thief, I can't touch that. But…

135
06:07.038 --> 06:09.407
corporate donations are fine to steal.

136
06:10.942 --> 06:13.444
That money was supposed to go away 30 years ago,

137
06:13.511 --> 06:14.946
so it shouldn't even exist.

138
06:15.580 --> 06:16.414
Good morals.

139
06:16.481 --> 06:18.883
How do we steal corporate donations?

140
06:23.254 --> 06:24.088
No idea.

141
06:24.155 --> 06:25.456
Find a way!

142
06:26.457 --> 06:28.626
How about the other kind of corporate donation?

143
06:28.693 --> 06:29.794
Fundraising parties.

144
06:29.861 --> 06:32.096
Like in foreign dramas,

145
06:32.163 --> 06:35.366
with a fancy venue, gathering politicians and rich people,

146
06:35.433 --> 06:36.934
asking for donations?

147
06:37.001 --> 06:41.172
Nipponia is quite different. They sell the party tickets themselves.

148
06:41.239 --> 06:43.407
They're 20,000 yen each.

149
06:45.309 --> 06:46.677
Tell me.

150
06:46.744 --> 06:49.046
Corporate donations survived.

151
06:49.113 --> 06:53.317
But the names of donating companies became easier to see.

152
06:53.384 --> 06:57.154
Politicians wanted to keep collecting big money in secret, like before.

153
06:57.221 --> 06:59.957
Companies wanted to keep donating without the public knowing.

154
07:00.024 --> 07:02.159
Then someone had an idea.

155
07:02.226 --> 07:05.897
If people are spending money to buy tickets,

156
07:05.963 --> 07:08.432
maybe it won't count as corporate donations.

157
07:08.499 --> 07:12.103
-"We're selling party tickets!" -"We'll buy ten!"

158
07:12.169 --> 07:15.006
So selling party tickets became a way to collect donations.

159
07:15.072 --> 07:18.309
A uniquely Nipponian method became widespread.

160
07:18.376 --> 07:20.912
"This makes money easier to collect! We did it!"

161
07:20.978 --> 07:23.347
They think up all kinds of tricks.

162
07:23.414 --> 07:25.616
It's definitely worth stealing.

163
07:40.364 --> 07:42.266
Ta-da!

164
07:42.333 --> 07:43.367
Who are you?

165
07:43.434 --> 07:44.468
Master.

166
07:44.535 --> 07:46.103
-A thief? -Master!

167
07:46.170 --> 07:47.538
So you're a thief, right?

168
07:47.605 --> 07:50.341
You should change the lock. I opened it in 30 seconds.

169
07:51.409 --> 07:52.510
So she is the master.

170
07:53.978 --> 07:57.481
It's the Seika era, in the democratic country of Nipponia.

171
07:58.316 --> 08:01.686
A huge ruling party has held power for a long time.

172
08:02.787 --> 08:06.591
This once-rich country is now on the edge of a cliff.

173
08:06.657 --> 08:08.793
The gap between rich and poor keeps growing.

174
08:08.859 --> 08:11.195
Both blue-collar workers and yakuza have no money.

175
08:11.896 --> 08:18.536
In Nagatanicho, the center of this nation, a great thief has landed.

176
08:18.603 --> 08:21.038
Her surname is Kisaragi, her given name is Yayoi.

177
08:21.105 --> 08:23.474
Her target is a safe in the Chief Cabinet Secretary's office,

178
08:23.541 --> 08:25.109
in the Prime Minister's residence.

179
08:25.176 --> 08:27.478
That video… Did you make it?

180
08:27.545 --> 08:30.147
-I thought you did it… -What?

181
08:30.214 --> 08:33.718
These two are engaged in a behind-the-scenes battle!

182
08:34.485 --> 08:36.887
You did a great job persuading my wayward daughter.

183
08:36.954 --> 08:39.857
She seemed troubled, so it was fairly easy.

184
08:39.924 --> 08:41.225
Here you go.

185
08:42.226 --> 08:45.162
Who will emerge victorious in the next match?

186
08:46.464 --> 08:50.001
What she will steal is the dark money of politicians!

187
08:50.067 --> 08:51.736
PoliticaLupin!

188
08:56.240 --> 08:59.543
Wow! What a good view!

189
09:00.277 --> 09:01.979
You should hide your valuables.

190
09:02.046 --> 09:05.216
That person ignores all rules of sharing and takes everything.

191
09:05.282 --> 09:08.052
-Like Gian from Doraemon? -Why did Rui tell her my address?!

192
09:08.119 --> 09:10.454
This is a good hideout. I like it.

193
09:10.521 --> 09:11.856
Don't you dare like it. Get out.

194
09:11.922 --> 09:14.492
-Reverse psychology? You want me to stay? -I mean it!

195
09:14.558 --> 09:17.061
Yayoi. Sit down here.

196
09:17.561 --> 09:20.331
The three rules of the thief! One!

197
09:21.399 --> 09:23.534
-Don't aim for big targets. -Two!

198
09:23.601 --> 09:24.935
Work quickly.

199
09:25.002 --> 09:28.305
Yes. Steal quickly, don't go big.

200
09:28.372 --> 09:31.342
-Those "cabinet secret funds" or whatever… -Cabinet Reward Funds!

201
09:31.409 --> 09:33.911
Sure, I don't care how big that treasure is.

202
09:33.978 --> 09:37.014
Just steal 50 million from that Miyabi woman and run.

203
09:37.081 --> 09:40.051
Fifty million is the yearly payment, and she doesn't have it on hand.

204
09:40.117 --> 09:43.654
Her family is rich, right? Just break in and steal.

205
09:43.721 --> 09:47.491
I'm not like you, Master, who steals from anyone. I'm a great thief.

206
09:47.558 --> 09:50.361
Wow! A great thief?!

207
09:51.362 --> 09:55.700
We're only small-time thieves. What are you saying?

208
09:56.367 --> 09:58.102
I'm going to work.

209
09:58.169 --> 10:00.438
Nagatanicho? Take me with you.

210
10:00.971 --> 10:03.307
Outsiders without an introduction can't enter.

211
10:06.844 --> 10:09.747
I came to see her, and she's so cold.

212
10:10.548 --> 10:11.449
What's the third rule?

213
10:13.084 --> 10:14.652
You said "three rules."

214
10:15.419 --> 10:16.787
Do what you want.

215
10:21.258 --> 10:23.694
Yes, about her salary…

216
10:23.761 --> 10:25.563
This is the office of Diet member Miyabi Kobayakawa.

217
10:25.629 --> 10:28.566
Why do we have to pay her 50 million yen?

218
10:28.632 --> 10:31.001
-It's our taxes! -Yes, you're right.

219
10:31.068 --> 10:33.571
It's tax-based, just like you said.

220
10:33.637 --> 10:36.440
But the 50 million includes expenses.

221
10:36.507 --> 10:38.876
Rent for the district office, equipment,

222
10:38.943 --> 10:41.412
and salaries for her secretaries, in this case, four.

223
10:41.479 --> 10:44.281
Many other political activities cost money, and then there's tax…

224
10:44.348 --> 10:46.984
She only gets to take home about 400,000 yen!

225
10:47.051 --> 10:51.489
We do need a law change to separate pay and expenses, like other countries…

226
10:53.224 --> 10:55.226
"Why is it so hard to understand?"

227
10:55.292 --> 10:59.230
They want to keep it vague so it can be used any way.

228
10:59.296 --> 11:01.098
Those politicians, I tell you.

229
11:01.966 --> 11:03.000
All right!

230
11:03.067 --> 11:04.802
Thank you for your opinion. Excuse me.

231
11:04.869 --> 11:06.937
-Excuse me! -Excuse me!

232
11:08.139 --> 11:12.376
Thank you for calling. Our phone service has ended for today.

233
11:12.443 --> 11:14.044
Lots of emails are coming in.

234
11:14.111 --> 11:16.547
Our social media is on fire too.

235
11:16.614 --> 11:17.848
Why is it this bad?

236
11:17.915 --> 11:20.151
The 750-yen Dolce drink Miyabi had.

237
11:20.217 --> 11:24.588
People attack it as her being out of touch with ordinary people.

238
11:25.156 --> 11:26.190
Did you tell Miyabi?

239
11:26.257 --> 11:28.559
She'll lose motivation. I said it's just a bit of backlash.

240
11:29.126 --> 11:30.261
That's good.

241
11:30.828 --> 11:35.232
"Miyabi doesn't drink Dolce lattes every day,

242
11:35.299 --> 11:38.369
it's just an occasional treat.

243
11:38.435 --> 11:40.905
To reward herself."

244
11:42.206 --> 11:44.108
"From her secretaries."

245
11:47.745 --> 11:48.979
Post!

246
11:50.714 --> 11:51.549
Welcome back.

247
11:51.615 --> 11:53.083
-I'm back. -Hello.

248
11:53.884 --> 11:56.287
I'm craving something sweet. I'll go buy a Dolce latte.

249
11:56.353 --> 11:58.589
-Huh? -Hold on!

250
11:59.156 --> 12:00.758
Maybe you can get another sweet treat.

251
12:00.825 --> 12:02.526
Maybe there are some at the party?

252
12:02.593 --> 12:04.361
-Party? -Yes, today.

253
12:04.428 --> 12:08.499
The Secretary-General told me to attend. But I want to skip it.

254
12:09.033 --> 12:09.934
Let's attend it!

255
12:10.734 --> 12:12.036
We can learn a lot! Right?

256
12:12.102 --> 12:13.037
Yeah!

257
12:13.103 --> 12:14.138
-Let's go! -My Dolce latte…

258
12:14.205 --> 12:15.139
-No! -No!

259
12:15.206 --> 12:16.707
SHIRO KOBAYAKAWA'S POLITICS WORKSHOP

260
12:16.774 --> 12:18.042
I'm Hayate Kobayakawa.

261
12:18.709 --> 12:20.678
Okay, I have received your information.

262
12:20.744 --> 12:23.380
-Your company name, please? -Here.

263
12:23.447 --> 12:26.317
-Please proceed to the left. -Thank you.

264
12:26.383 --> 12:28.619
No one is handing over cash.

265
12:29.553 --> 12:32.623
The law was changed after that slush fund scandal.

266
12:32.690 --> 12:34.792
Slush fund scandal? What was that?

267
12:34.859 --> 12:36.427
85 KOKUJI PARTY POLITICIANS DIDN'T SPECIFY THEIR INCOME

268
12:36.493 --> 12:38.429
At the Kokuji Party's faction parties,

269
12:38.495 --> 12:41.365
some party members took cash to avoid a paper trail.

270
12:41.432 --> 12:45.002
The Kokuji Party "took action" by dissolving its factions.

271
12:45.069 --> 12:47.271
The laws about parties were changed too.

272
12:47.338 --> 12:50.808
NO HANDING OVER CASH MUST LEAVE BEHIND BANK ACCOUNT INFO

273
12:50.875 --> 12:54.411
What? So they can't have slush funds? So there's no money to steal?

274
12:54.478 --> 12:57.882
In the faction days, there were even quotas for how many tickets to sell.

275
12:57.948 --> 12:59.850
Like an unpopular comedian?

276
12:59.917 --> 13:01.385
Maybe a theater troupe?

277
13:01.452 --> 13:05.356
Hey, there's a waiting room. Let's drop off our bags.

278
13:05.422 --> 13:06.657
Okay.

279
13:06.724 --> 13:08.826
WAITING ROOM

280
13:21.939 --> 13:23.307
I'm hungry.

281
13:24.308 --> 13:25.276
Same.

282
13:25.776 --> 13:28.112
Oh, how you've grown!

283
13:28.178 --> 13:29.413
I still have a lot to do.

284
13:29.480 --> 13:31.916
I have known Mr. Shiro for many years.

285
13:31.982 --> 13:34.151
Don't post photos taken on social media.

286
13:34.218 --> 13:36.086
Please keep them as personal mementos.

287
13:36.153 --> 13:38.489
I've heard a lot about you.

288
13:38.555 --> 13:41.825
You also have such a wonderful daughter, Mr. Shiro.

289
13:41.892 --> 13:43.294
I envy you.

290
13:43.360 --> 13:44.828
I'm Miyabi Kobayakawa.

291
13:44.895 --> 13:45.930
I'm Yoshino.

292
13:45.996 --> 13:47.998
-Nice to meet you. -Nice to meet you.

293
13:48.766 --> 13:50.000
It's fairly plain.

294
13:51.068 --> 13:52.636
Only four kinds of drinks.

295
13:52.703 --> 13:55.005
We try to spend as little as possible.

296
13:55.072 --> 13:58.575
A secretary's skills are shown in the profit rate he can bring in.

297
13:59.576 --> 14:01.345
It's better if few guests actually come.

298
14:01.412 --> 14:03.781
If we sell 1,000 tickets and only 200 come,

299
14:03.847 --> 14:05.716
we profit from 800 people entirely.

300
14:05.783 --> 14:08.018
It's like a club trying to earn money!

301
14:08.085 --> 14:09.453
-That's it! -Yeah.

302
14:09.520 --> 14:13.390
Well, our purpose is to gather donations, so that's how it is.

303
14:13.457 --> 14:17.127
Companies write it off as entertainment expenses and save taxes.

304
14:17.194 --> 14:18.095
A win-win.

305
14:19.997 --> 14:24.335
In the general election, we faced a headwind.

306
14:24.401 --> 14:26.503
But there was also happy news.

307
14:26.570 --> 14:28.806
Both of my children

308
14:28.872 --> 14:31.208
have become Diet members.

309
14:32.309 --> 14:33.911
Join me, Hayate, Miyabi.

310
14:35.446 --> 14:36.447
Come on.

311
14:46.290 --> 14:49.159
Everyone is waiting. Come on.

312
14:50.394 --> 14:54.098
SHIRO KOBAYAKAWA'S POLITICS WORKSHOP

313
14:54.164 --> 14:55.733
Miyabi spoke out

314
14:56.934 --> 14:59.269
quite openly, on TV.

315
15:00.204 --> 15:04.608
And many of you have scolded her for causing distrust in politics.

316
15:04.675 --> 15:08.379
I lost my wife early

317
15:08.445 --> 15:12.316
and raised my children alone as a man.

318
15:12.383 --> 15:16.320
Miyabi is a girl, so I spoiled her.

319
15:16.387 --> 15:18.922
But from now on I won't be lenient.

320
15:19.490 --> 15:21.558
I'll be super strict with her!

321
15:22.926 --> 15:24.762
Together as parent and children,

322
15:24.828 --> 15:28.999
we'll risk our lives to improve the Kokuji Party.

323
15:30.334 --> 15:34.638
The Kokuji Party will keep a firm hold on Nipponia's future.

324
15:34.705 --> 15:36.807
We'll steer her onwards!

325
15:39.443 --> 15:42.646
Steady as she goes, Nipponia!

326
15:43.213 --> 15:46.016
-Steady as she goes, Nipponia! -Steady as she goes, Nipponia!

327
15:57.227 --> 15:58.829
Mr. Kobayakawa!

328
16:02.466 --> 16:04.034
Thank you!

329
16:04.101 --> 16:05.402
Thank you very much.

330
16:08.238 --> 16:12.810
Big companies all support them, and they control politics with money.

331
16:13.977 --> 16:16.213
Anyone who resists is a fool.

332
16:17.881 --> 16:19.416
Are you giving up?

333
16:22.419 --> 16:24.388
We have to be able to gather this many people

334
16:24.922 --> 16:27.925
with our own power,

335
16:27.991 --> 16:30.194
or we can't beat Dad, you know?

336
16:40.737 --> 16:41.872
Ms. Kisaragi.

337
16:43.273 --> 16:46.143
I'm sorry about the other day.

338
16:47.911 --> 16:50.481
I regret the Ponz Next matter.

339
16:51.315 --> 16:54.284
I should have done more.

340
16:55.119 --> 16:59.490
Talking with you gives me many insights. It's stimulating.

341
17:00.657 --> 17:05.362
I would like to talk more. Let's have a deep conversation.

342
17:06.864 --> 17:07.998
A deep one?

343
17:10.934 --> 17:14.404
If you like, shall we have dinner sometime?

344
17:14.471 --> 17:18.742
I'm happy to hear that. I want to know more about you too.

345
17:20.844 --> 17:22.279
Which companies

346
17:23.480 --> 17:27.151
are you receiving political donations from?

347
17:28.652 --> 17:31.889
The Ministry of Economy gets funds from energy and heavy industry.

348
17:31.955 --> 17:34.525
But you're from the Finance Ministry, Mr. Natsume.

349
17:34.591 --> 17:37.027
You leaned in this close, just to ask me that?

350
17:37.694 --> 17:39.563
Well, it is a deep conversation.

351
17:41.064 --> 17:43.167
Are you sure this is the right level for you?

352
17:47.104 --> 17:49.640
Please teach me…

353
17:51.375 --> 17:55.212
How to make slush funds at parties.

354
17:55.279 --> 17:59.349
I don't hold parties, as a principle.

355
18:01.952 --> 18:04.454
You sure like money talk, Ms. Kisaragi.

356
18:07.658 --> 18:08.659
I love it!

357
18:09.526 --> 18:11.395
Not as much as politicians do though.

358
18:11.461 --> 18:14.431
Politicians don't talk money openly.

359
18:15.432 --> 18:18.035
-Thieves don't do it openly either. -Thieves?

360
18:18.101 --> 18:19.903
Politicians and thieves are similar.

361
18:19.970 --> 18:20.938
You think so?

362
18:21.772 --> 18:25.809
Every year, they steal a cup of coffee from every citizen.

363
18:25.876 --> 18:26.944
I see.

364
18:28.245 --> 18:30.280
The party grants?

365
18:30.347 --> 18:32.249
Those politicians…

366
18:33.083 --> 18:36.019
probably don't think those 250 yen is a big deal at all.

367
18:37.888 --> 18:39.022
There you are.

368
18:39.990 --> 18:43.894
Hey, we should hold a party too.

369
18:44.461 --> 18:45.229
Huh?

370
18:45.295 --> 18:47.931
If we don't have this much money and this many people,

371
18:47.998 --> 18:51.535
we can't change anything, so shouldn't we aim for it?

372
18:52.402 --> 18:54.905
How can we collect corporate donations like crazy?

373
18:56.206 --> 18:57.040
Wait.

374
18:57.908 --> 18:59.409
Am I saying something strange?

375
19:00.310 --> 19:04.047
You're talking like a true Kokuji Party member.

376
19:05.182 --> 19:06.350
Yeah.

377
19:06.416 --> 19:08.151
Then I'll place my bet here.

378
19:08.218 --> 19:10.354
Germany seems to have money to steal too.

379
19:10.420 --> 19:13.123
They say its political transparency is pretty high.

380
19:13.190 --> 19:14.625
-Welcome back. -Welcome back.

381
19:15.559 --> 19:17.494
You're friends already! What are you doing?

382
19:17.561 --> 19:18.762
Stole anything?

383
19:20.697 --> 19:23.066
There wasn't any cash to steal.

384
19:23.133 --> 19:26.903
Well, Shiro Kobayakawa has no need for slush funds.

385
19:26.970 --> 19:30.807
So only struggling Diet Members need to have slush funds?

386
19:30.874 --> 19:31.708
How sad.

387
19:31.775 --> 19:34.678
Even when struggling, they'll always have food to eat.

388
19:34.745 --> 19:36.179
They're on a different Babel.

389
19:37.247 --> 19:39.549
They're on a different Babel!

390
19:40.751 --> 19:42.919
Without a common language, we can't understand each other!

391
19:42.986 --> 19:44.554
I haven't heard that phrase for ages.

392
19:44.621 --> 19:48.158
When I used it as an adult, nobody knew it, and I was shocked.

393
19:48.225 --> 19:50.794
Back then, you were just a small kid, Rui.

394
19:50.861 --> 19:52.529
What? You three are childhood friends?

395
19:52.596 --> 19:56.366
We lived in the same place. We called it Crystal House.

396
19:56.433 --> 19:58.001
It doesn't deserve such a cool name.

397
19:58.068 --> 20:00.871
Well, you left really early, Kohaku. You went bad.

398
20:00.937 --> 20:02.839
Who could stay in such a shabby place?

399
20:02.906 --> 20:04.308
A house that raises thieves?

400
20:04.374 --> 20:05.509
The opposite.

401
20:06.476 --> 20:10.047
The House got closed, and I became Kohaku's apprentice in thievery.

402
20:10.113 --> 20:11.415
I'm a great thief though.

403
20:11.481 --> 20:13.583
Do you need to keep telling yourself that?

404
20:13.650 --> 20:14.751
I do.

405
20:18.722 --> 20:19.723
You…

406
20:21.124 --> 20:23.327
You aren't dreaming of strange things, right?

407
20:24.594 --> 20:26.196
Why expect anything from politicians?

408
20:26.263 --> 20:27.931
I don't expect anything.

409
20:27.998 --> 20:30.734
We know it in our bones, don't we?

410
20:34.037 --> 20:35.005
I know.

411
20:35.072 --> 20:37.007
Do you really?

412
20:40.811 --> 20:41.978
Even now…

413
20:44.715 --> 20:46.083
I can still hear it.

414
20:47.918 --> 20:49.720
The sound of 250 yen.

415
21:05.435 --> 21:08.004
Fifty… Here.

416
21:16.079 --> 21:20.150
Every big company already has a set place to donate to.

417
21:20.951 --> 21:25.322
Maybe companies for women are untouched. Let's make a list and visit them.

418
21:25.389 --> 21:26.690
Please do.

419
21:26.757 --> 21:30.961
So secretaries do sales too. That's why we need several.

420
21:31.027 --> 21:33.930
We need many secretaries to do sales,

421
21:33.997 --> 21:37.534
and then we do sales to pay for secretaries?

422
21:38.668 --> 21:41.838
Last weekend, I went to Chiba alone, right?

423
21:41.905 --> 21:44.408
I did a round of local assembly gatherings.

424
21:44.474 --> 21:45.909
Shouldn't have gone empty-handed.

425
21:45.976 --> 21:47.511
-Like, bring some sweets? -Hard cash.

426
21:47.577 --> 21:48.412
-What?! -What?!

427
21:48.478 --> 21:50.547
You have to bring cash with you, at those times.

428
21:50.614 --> 21:51.715
-What?! -What?!

429
21:51.782 --> 21:53.817
"Oh? You're empty-handed?"

430
21:53.884 --> 21:55.952
"Well, it can't be helped.

431
21:56.019 --> 21:58.755
Your Diet Member is still a young lady after all."

432
21:59.790 --> 22:03.226
"We can't help you in the next election, unless…"

433
22:03.293 --> 22:05.562
They joked like that, and I was like, sorry!

434
22:05.629 --> 22:09.466
I'm just a rookie and all, please forgive my rudeness.

435
22:09.533 --> 22:10.901
I had to joke around.

436
22:10.967 --> 22:11.935
You're great, Momo.

437
22:12.002 --> 22:13.270
I couldn't have handled it.

438
22:13.336 --> 22:15.906
Local members control the elections.

439
22:15.972 --> 22:18.208
But if that's the case, no amount of money would be enough!

440
22:18.275 --> 22:22.412
The more money a politician has, the more powerful he becomes.

441
22:23.613 --> 22:24.948
No wonder…

442
22:25.015 --> 22:28.819
that 30-year-old promise was forgotten.

443
22:32.022 --> 22:34.758
I'll go donate to the vending machine.

444
22:37.694 --> 22:39.396
She was talking about party grants, right?

445
22:39.463 --> 22:40.363
What?

446
22:40.430 --> 22:42.799
You know, the 250 yen per citizen.

447
22:42.866 --> 22:43.800
Yes, that.

448
22:45.535 --> 22:51.641
Nipponia's population is 125 million people…

449
22:52.175 --> 22:56.279
Times 250 yen…

450
22:56.346 --> 22:57.380
That would be…

451
22:59.516 --> 23:02.385
About 31.2 billion,

452
23:02.953 --> 23:06.289
times 30 years…

453
23:06.356 --> 23:07.424
Wow.

454
23:09.960 --> 23:11.461
ABOUT ONE TRILLION YEN

455
23:16.099 --> 23:16.933
ELECTION REFORM LEAGUE

456
23:17.000 --> 23:21.004
Thanks to you joining as a sponsor, we've gathered eight people.

457
23:21.071 --> 23:22.138
Thank you.

458
23:22.906 --> 23:24.274
You owe me one.

459
23:24.341 --> 23:25.208
Yes.

460
23:26.877 --> 23:29.379
On another matter,

461
23:30.080 --> 23:34.150
do opposition parties not hold fundraising parties much?

462
23:34.217 --> 23:36.253
Even if they do, people don't attend.

463
23:36.319 --> 23:39.589
Companies want to meet ruling-party politicians.

464
23:39.656 --> 23:44.594
They donate regularly because they want favorable laws for their industry.

465
23:44.661 --> 23:45.529
Right.

466
23:46.196 --> 23:48.498
How do you feel about corporate donations?

467
23:48.565 --> 23:50.734
I'm against them. But…

468
23:50.800 --> 23:53.436
Opposition parties are also hesitant to abolish them,

469
23:53.503 --> 23:57.340
because they get group donations from labor unions.

470
23:57.874 --> 24:01.478
If only we could get by with individual donations only.

471
24:01.545 --> 24:05.148
Do individual donations make fraud and favoritism less likely?

472
24:05.215 --> 24:09.653
Well, sort of. Since the cap is 1.5 million yen.

473
24:09.719 --> 24:12.489
Then we could stop corporate donations and use only individuals.

474
24:12.556 --> 24:14.057
About that…

475
24:14.591 --> 24:16.726
Unlike Christian countries,

476
24:16.793 --> 24:19.529
a culture of donation hasn't taken root in Nipponia.

477
24:19.596 --> 24:22.566
The citizens aren't very interested in politics,

478
24:22.632 --> 24:25.168
so they don't donate much.

479
24:30.507 --> 24:32.042
Working in a place like this?

480
24:32.108 --> 24:34.344
Hey, do you know any party information?

481
24:34.411 --> 24:35.545
Party?

482
24:38.782 --> 24:40.216
What do you want to know?

483
24:43.053 --> 24:46.823
After the slush fund scandal, they stopped posting info about fundraising parties.

484
24:46.890 --> 24:51.027
But I hear such parties are held somewhere every day. So, who would know about them?

485
24:51.094 --> 24:52.295
I know one such party.

486
24:54.297 --> 24:55.799
An especially big one.

487
24:58.201 --> 24:59.202
A seminar?

488
24:59.269 --> 25:01.972
It's just a different name for a political fundraising party.

489
25:02.038 --> 25:03.373
That's how it's done?

490
25:03.440 --> 25:08.211
It seems the secretaries go around and sell tickets in person.

491
25:10.213 --> 25:14.751
If you can be there that day, we prefer you pay on the day.

492
25:15.652 --> 25:18.121
They want it in cash? Is that allowed?

493
25:18.188 --> 25:20.090
-It seems so. -I see.

494
25:20.156 --> 25:24.127
The revised law has an exception clause.

495
25:24.694 --> 25:27.497
"I happened to be there that day, so I paid in cash."

496
25:27.564 --> 25:30.500
That kind of excuse works. It's a loophole.

497
25:30.567 --> 25:31.635
I see…

498
25:31.701 --> 25:35.405
-Share this with Yayoi Kisaragi. -What?

499
25:35.472 --> 25:38.074
She wanted to learn about parties.

500
25:38.141 --> 25:40.310
Don't ever say it's from me.

501
25:40.377 --> 25:44.781
Just casually tell her about it. You're good at that, right?

502
25:45.281 --> 25:47.417
Don't say anything extra.

503
25:49.319 --> 25:50.787
Was it helpful info?

504
25:51.454 --> 25:52.656
It's great. Thank you.

505
25:54.290 --> 25:55.725
I'm good at this.

506
25:56.926 --> 25:58.261
Saying things I shouldn't.

507
25:59.329 --> 26:02.265
My boss, Masakado, is amazing.

508
26:03.500 --> 26:06.870
He got into Tokyo University straight out of school, entered the Finance Ministry,

509
26:06.936 --> 26:09.606
got promoted young, and went into politics.

510
26:09.673 --> 26:11.508
-He's on a different Babel. -Huh?

511
26:11.574 --> 26:12.609
Go on.

512
26:14.144 --> 26:17.814
He has no girlfriend, no divorce history. Please take care of him.

513
26:19.649 --> 26:23.319
And he absolutely does not do slush funds.

514
26:25.789 --> 26:28.992
He told me before that 250 yen is not cheap.

515
26:30.894 --> 26:32.429
I mean, let's say

516
26:33.329 --> 26:37.033
I borrow 250 yen from a friend and forget to return it.

517
26:37.100 --> 26:38.668
Who would get mad over that?

518
26:38.735 --> 26:43.373
Idiot. You're in the wrong. Go and apologize to your friend.

519
26:43.440 --> 26:45.041
It's just 250 yen!

520
26:45.108 --> 26:46.409
In this world,

521
26:46.476 --> 26:50.914
there are people whose phones get cut off because they don't have 250 yen.

522
26:50.980 --> 26:54.684
So they can't ask anyone for help, and they die.

523
26:54.751 --> 26:56.086
There are people like that.

524
26:56.152 --> 26:57.187
That's exaggerated.

525
26:57.253 --> 26:59.522
There are such people.

526
27:00.423 --> 27:02.926
You deal with money in hundreds of trillions, you know.

527
27:03.526 --> 27:07.130
The Finance Ministry's job is to make the budget make sense.

528
27:07.197 --> 27:09.365
We look at statistics and numbers.

529
27:09.432 --> 27:12.135
But politicians can't be only that.

530
27:14.637 --> 27:19.476
I don't want people who say "it's only 250 yen" to become politicians.

531
27:22.879 --> 27:27.016
He became a politician to look at people, not numbers.

532
27:27.083 --> 27:29.519
-That's the kind of man he is. -That's enough.

533
27:31.154 --> 27:35.058
I'm glad there are still people like him.

534
27:40.330 --> 27:42.065
Excuse me, then.

535
27:48.471 --> 27:50.774
Even with people like him around,

536
27:50.840 --> 27:54.010
nothing has changed in 30 years.

537
27:54.911 --> 27:56.846
That means it's okay to steal.

538
27:57.447 --> 27:58.915
POLITICAL SEMINAR IN TOKYO

539
27:58.982 --> 28:00.416
There's a rumor in Nagatanicho

540
28:00.483 --> 28:03.753
that former Kobayakawa faction leaders are meeting.

541
28:03.820 --> 28:06.322
A leadership change may be near.

542
28:08.925 --> 28:12.629
But Kazamatsuri doesn't plan to step down.

543
28:13.596 --> 28:15.298
Can he keep hanging on?

544
28:15.799 --> 28:17.500
I FOUND TREASURE MONEY WILL GATHER AT THE VENUE

545
28:17.567 --> 28:20.670
POLITICAL SEMINAR

546
28:20.737 --> 28:23.640
The Prime Minister is coming to Tabata's party?

547
28:24.140 --> 28:26.476
Tabata is just small-fry, isn't he?

548
28:27.343 --> 28:28.878
They're from the same hometown.

549
28:28.945 --> 28:30.046
He's his junior.

550
28:35.718 --> 28:37.086
POLITICAL SEMINAR

551
28:37.153 --> 28:38.221
Huh?

552
28:43.393 --> 28:45.595
These flyers look the same but are actually different.

553
28:47.764 --> 28:52.702
POLITICAL SEMINAR

554
28:54.270 --> 28:56.639
The bank account for transfers!

555
28:57.140 --> 28:58.474
RISUNO BANK 1043756

556
28:58.541 --> 29:02.712
This is Tabata's account, and this is Kazamatsuri's account.

557
29:02.779 --> 29:04.080
After the slush fund scandal,

558
29:04.147 --> 29:06.082
Prime Ministers cannot hold parties anymore.

559
29:06.149 --> 29:08.351
But this way, Kazamatsuri can collect funds too.

560
29:08.418 --> 29:09.953
They're even smarter than us thieves!

561
29:10.019 --> 29:12.255
He needs money for the next election.

562
29:12.889 --> 29:16.526
We must take this shady money for sure.

563
29:16.593 --> 29:18.328
The venue is the same as last time.

564
29:18.394 --> 29:21.698
The cash should be carried to the waiting room.

565
29:21.764 --> 29:22.765
Timing?

566
29:25.969 --> 29:28.338
We'll set up cameras

567
29:29.806 --> 29:31.040
and watch the timing.

568
29:31.107 --> 29:33.009
Where will you enter from?

569
29:33.610 --> 29:38.281
The front. I asked Miyabi if I could join, to observe.

570
29:38.348 --> 29:42.485
May I attend the next party too?

571
29:42.552 --> 29:44.187
Of course.

572
29:45.054 --> 29:48.191
-Give Tabata a support speech. -Yes.

573
29:48.258 --> 29:50.894
Mr. Shiro's daughter… Miyabi?

574
29:51.828 --> 29:53.730
She also wants to attend, with her secretary.

575
29:53.796 --> 29:55.732
Tabata sure is popular.

576
29:55.798 --> 29:57.300
It's all thanks to you, Prime Minister.

577
29:58.801 --> 30:00.069
Sure.

578
30:01.871 --> 30:03.506
I think it will be lively.

579
30:04.908 --> 30:09.145
Please do not broadcast today's gathering on social media.

580
30:09.212 --> 30:10.914
Your invitation, please.

581
30:10.980 --> 30:13.383
Please do not post any photos or videos on social media…

582
30:13.449 --> 30:15.218
Thank you.

583
30:15.885 --> 30:17.954
-This way, please. -We ask for your cooperation.

584
30:18.021 --> 30:20.390
-Let's go to the waiting room. -I'll go drop our bags.

585
30:20.456 --> 30:22.992
-Are you sure? Thank you. -Go greet people.

586
30:23.726 --> 30:24.560
Okay.

587
30:24.627 --> 30:26.329
-Thank you. -It's fine.

588
30:40.977 --> 30:42.045
Signal good.

589
30:42.111 --> 30:43.313
Position good.

590
30:59.896 --> 31:01.631
Someone is hiding in the hallway.

591
31:02.632 --> 31:04.033
There's a camera in the room too.

592
31:04.100 --> 31:08.204
There wasn't before. Someone other than us is watching.

593
31:09.205 --> 31:10.573
Have we been marked?

594
31:10.640 --> 31:13.977
Prime Minister Kazamatsuri, a senior from my hometown of Iwate,

595
31:14.043 --> 31:16.746
has helped me many times

596
31:16.813 --> 31:19.115
in my political career.

597
31:19.182 --> 31:20.616
I still have lots to learn…

598
31:20.683 --> 31:22.752
Who is it? Who would…

599
31:27.924 --> 31:30.293
I know one such party. An especially big one.

600
31:30.360 --> 31:31.461
Parties?

601
31:32.996 --> 31:34.564
It's Natsume.

602
31:42.739 --> 31:45.942
WAITING ROOM

603
31:53.449 --> 31:55.885
The treasure arrives. They're counting.

604
31:55.952 --> 31:57.954
Tabata joined national politics from Iwate,

605
31:58.021 --> 32:01.057
and is now Chair of the Economy, Trade and Industry Committee,

606
32:01.124 --> 32:02.225
working for Nipponia's future…

607
32:09.966 --> 32:11.667
They put two million yen into each white envelope

608
32:11.734 --> 32:12.835
and placed them in a box.

609
32:13.770 --> 32:17.440
I myself will try not to lose to Tabata's efforts…

610
32:17.507 --> 32:19.809
What do we do? Do we call it off?

611
32:21.310 --> 32:22.311
What do we do?

612
32:23.012 --> 32:24.814
Wait, wait a moment.

613
32:25.548 --> 32:28.518
For the sake of Nipponia, I…

614
32:28.584 --> 32:31.020
I will give my whole life!

615
32:31.087 --> 32:34.090
Do we stop? What do we do?

616
32:34.157 --> 32:38.194
Everyone, join me! To pray for Nipponia's success!

617
32:38.261 --> 32:40.897
Steady as she goes, Nipponia!

618
32:41.631 --> 32:43.699
-Steady as she goes, Nipponia! -Steady as she goes, Nipponia!

619
32:43.766 --> 32:45.868
-Steady as she goes, Nipponia! -Steady as she goes, Nipponia!

620
32:50.473 --> 32:52.475
Thank you, Mr. Tabata.

621
33:18.534 --> 33:19.402
Let's do it.

622
33:20.169 --> 33:22.438
They're amateurs. There are few of them.

623
33:24.107 --> 33:27.477
Understood. In two minutes. Ready…

624
33:27.543 --> 33:30.880
Three, two, one.

625
33:31.414 --> 33:33.816
TIMER

626
33:33.883 --> 33:37.420
Please enjoy the drinks and food.

627
33:37.487 --> 33:41.624
Meanwhile, a word from Diet member Miyabi Kobayakawa.

628
33:42.592 --> 33:46.262
I'm Miyabi Kobayakawa from Chiba District 17.

629
33:46.329 --> 33:47.497
Well…

630
33:48.865 --> 33:50.700
I haven't prepared anything.

631
33:51.601 --> 33:52.735
All right…

632
33:54.470 --> 33:57.073
I'll tell you a story about 250 yen.

633
33:58.107 --> 34:00.009
Do you know halo-halo?

634
34:00.810 --> 34:05.214
A Filipino dessert of shaved ice with fruit and cream.

635
34:05.281 --> 34:09.619
When I was in fourth grade, I saw a banner in front of a convenience store.

636
34:09.685 --> 34:11.454
"Halo-halo, 250 yen."

637
34:13.089 --> 34:15.391
I begged my father for it.

638
34:15.958 --> 34:19.095
He said, "It's embarrassing to buy convenience store stuff. Stop it."

639
34:20.329 --> 34:22.798
He took me to a parlor in Ginza.

640
34:24.500 --> 34:28.504
There, they served a splendid 2,500-yen parfait,

641
34:29.472 --> 34:32.775
with a big piece of melon on top, and it smelled so good.

642
34:34.410 --> 34:37.980
I thought, "Oh, of course this tastes better."

643
34:39.182 --> 34:42.785
But if I had eaten the halo-halo then,

644
34:43.386 --> 34:50.293
if I had known a life where you treat a 250-yen snack like a feast,

645
34:51.327 --> 34:55.131
I might have become a person who understands many people's feelings,

646
34:55.197 --> 34:58.801
a person fit to be a politician.

647
34:59.368 --> 35:03.472
What's disappointing about me now

648
35:03.539 --> 35:07.710
is that my imagination about 250 yen is only that small.

649
35:08.911 --> 35:12.048
Sorry. What I want to talk about is…

650
35:12.114 --> 35:13.883
TIME'S UP

651
35:18.654 --> 35:21.457
Everyone, please calm down and wait.

652
35:22.024 --> 35:25.528
Everyone, please stay calm and stay where you are.

653
35:39.075 --> 35:40.610
Oh, camera function…

654
35:43.012 --> 35:46.115
Not this one…

655
36:06.636 --> 36:07.637
Found it!

656
36:09.005 --> 36:12.108
Excuse me! Excuse me!

657
36:22.518 --> 36:25.021
Did you find out who the culprit is?

658
36:25.087 --> 36:28.991
Not yet. But if we catch them, we'll be able to clear your suspicions.

659
36:36.465 --> 36:38.234
It hurts! They got us!

660
36:39.568 --> 36:41.871
Hey! Anyone there?! Darn it!

661
36:42.505 --> 36:44.874
Someone! Someone!

662
36:45.875 --> 36:47.009
EMERGENCY POWER

663
36:47.076 --> 36:48.244
I caught the culprit!

664
36:48.311 --> 36:49.345
Let me go!

665
36:49.945 --> 36:52.081
Oh, so it was you?!

666
36:52.148 --> 36:53.849
-Of course not! -Huh?

667
36:55.584 --> 36:56.819
Mr. Natsume?!

668
37:05.394 --> 37:06.829
What is this?

669
37:08.698 --> 37:11.200
Excuse me, but who are you?

670
37:13.035 --> 37:15.738
My name is Kimura.

671
37:17.239 --> 37:19.175
-May I look inside your bag? -What?

672
37:19.241 --> 37:20.776
-We lost a lot of money. -What?

673
37:20.843 --> 37:23.512
Are you with the police? You creep me out…

674
37:27.183 --> 37:30.319
Give me your business card. I'll write about this online.

675
37:30.386 --> 37:33.989
I'm very sorry. I hope you can forgive me.

676
37:34.056 --> 37:35.224
Excuse me.

677
37:44.800 --> 37:46.836
Ta-da.

678
37:49.338 --> 37:50.272
I failed.

679
37:50.339 --> 37:51.173
Why?

680
37:51.240 --> 37:52.608
Kohaku beat me to it!

681
38:10.292 --> 38:14.530
Everyone, we've confirmed the venue's power is restored…

682
38:14.597 --> 38:16.098
When did you return here?

683
38:16.866 --> 38:18.367
I was here all this time.

684
38:18.434 --> 38:20.503
It was so dark and scary.

685
38:20.569 --> 38:23.005
Let's watch a new commercial for the Kokuji Party…

686
38:23.072 --> 38:27.276
Excuse me? My talk isn't done yet. I'll keep it short and sharp.

687
38:28.477 --> 38:29.645
It's 250 yen.

688
38:31.247 --> 38:34.216
That 250 yen per citizen

689
38:34.283 --> 38:36.886
is the source of party grants.

690
38:37.453 --> 38:38.888
Only 250 yen.

691
38:38.954 --> 38:43.058
But over 30 years, it's one trillion yen.

692
38:43.826 --> 38:47.663
Could you ignore a one-trillion-yen contract with a client?

693
38:47.730 --> 38:48.998
Normally you couldn't.

694
38:49.865 --> 38:55.237
Yet corporate and group donations continue, and parties like this continue.

695
38:55.805 --> 38:57.173
We politicians

696
38:58.107 --> 39:01.677
made a one-trillion-yen contract with the citizens.

697
39:01.744 --> 39:03.746
So why are we ignoring it?

698
39:04.713 --> 39:07.616
Isn't this what breeds distrust in politics?

699
39:08.784 --> 39:13.022
I will fulfill the promise from 30 years ago.

700
39:15.357 --> 39:18.627
I'll become that kind of politician. Thank you for listening.

701
39:24.266 --> 39:26.535
Youngsters sure have fresh ideas.

702
39:26.602 --> 39:27.803
Indeed.

703
39:33.742 --> 39:35.544
Switch on!

704
39:38.180 --> 39:40.950
-Oh, wow… -Oh, wow…

705
39:41.016 --> 39:43.252
Won't people say you're wasting money again?

706
39:43.319 --> 39:46.322
One cup is 55 yen, so it pays for itself in one year.

707
39:47.122 --> 39:50.025
A generic Dolce latte cup can be made for 100 yen.

708
39:51.060 --> 39:52.261
Don't post it on social media.

709
39:52.328 --> 39:54.730
No, let's publish everything on our official website. All the details.

710
39:54.797 --> 39:56.265
-Wait, we're going to do that? -Yes.

711
39:56.332 --> 39:58.767
That makes it easier to collect individual donations.

712
39:58.834 --> 40:00.102
Individual donations?

713
40:00.169 --> 40:03.305
These days people do online donations and crowdfunding,

714
40:03.372 --> 40:06.242
and it's quite easy, so I think we can do it too.

715
40:06.308 --> 40:10.479
We will join hands with citizens, rather than companies.

716
40:10.546 --> 40:11.780
Nice!

717
40:12.414 --> 40:14.016
If we make this a success story,

718
40:14.083 --> 40:19.822
maybe we can keep the 30-year-old promise to abolish corporate and group donations.

719
40:22.024 --> 40:23.526
That's great.

720
40:23.592 --> 40:25.427
-Oh, Yayoi. -Yes?

721
40:25.494 --> 40:28.497
-The 250 yen I borrowed. Thank you. -It's nothing.

722
40:42.478 --> 40:44.179
Where is my and Rui's share?

723
40:44.246 --> 40:45.281
Study fee.

724
40:45.347 --> 40:46.715
That again?!

725
40:47.316 --> 40:49.418
And Natsume saw your face too.

726
40:49.485 --> 40:51.787
Now he thinks I am the great thief!

727
40:52.721 --> 40:54.256
That's better for you too, isn't it?

728
40:55.691 --> 40:58.260
Maybe I'll go overseas.

729
40:59.261 --> 41:00.963
There's no dream here.

730
41:01.597 --> 41:03.365
And there are dreams over there?

731
41:08.337 --> 41:09.939
I guess there aren't any anywhere.

732
41:12.975 --> 41:15.544
The third rule for us thieves.

733
41:17.279 --> 41:19.014
Don't trust anyone.

734
41:20.783 --> 41:22.117
Don't forget it.

735
41:23.419 --> 41:26.555
Your compassion is your weakness.

736
41:26.622 --> 41:27.923
I know.

737
41:30.292 --> 41:32.261
-Do you? -Don't touch me!

738
41:32.828 --> 41:34.797
Hey, don't touch me!

739
41:34.863 --> 41:37.132
-Take this! -Hey, don't touch me!

740
41:37.199 --> 41:39.702
All the hidden camera lenses broke.

741
41:40.336 --> 41:42.738
The venue's security cameras too,

742
41:42.805 --> 41:45.441
so none of them got any worthwhile evidence.

743
41:46.008 --> 41:48.043
There was damage besides cash.

744
41:48.110 --> 41:52.247
For this party, the transfers were split.

745
41:53.015 --> 41:56.518
Between the Prime Minister's account and Tabata's account.

746
41:56.585 --> 41:58.020
RISUNO BANK

747
41:58.087 --> 41:59.221
But…

748
41:59.288 --> 42:02.057
the money they received was less than half of the expected amount.

749
42:02.124 --> 42:03.192
When we checked,

750
42:03.892 --> 42:07.129
there was a third flyer that no one remembered printing.

751
42:14.136 --> 42:16.939
RISUNO BANK

752
42:17.506 --> 42:18.574
Oh, wow!

753
42:18.641 --> 42:21.944
DEPOSIT RECORDS

754
42:22.011 --> 42:23.045
It's surpassed 20 million.

755
42:23.112 --> 42:26.248
So it was this easy to steal their money…

756
42:26.315 --> 42:29.084
YOSHIMASA TABATA

757
42:34.757 --> 42:36.558
TABATA - KAZAMATSURI

758
42:37.993 --> 42:38.861
KAZAMATSURI

759
42:40.696 --> 42:43.866
I only sneaked in and swapped the flyers!

760
42:44.366 --> 42:45.567
Why do you look dissatisfied?

761
42:45.634 --> 42:48.470
Won't this account be traced?

762
42:49.171 --> 42:52.207
There are over 7,800 party branches in the Kokuji Party alone.

763
42:52.274 --> 42:54.910
Political groups number in the tens of thousands,

764
42:54.977 --> 42:57.713
and nobody can keep track of them all.

765
42:57.780 --> 42:59.181
I see.

766
42:59.248 --> 43:02.718
Also, "someone" will do their best so this account won't be investigated.

767
43:04.053 --> 43:05.254
Who?

768
43:07.790 --> 43:10.259
I checked this account,

769
43:10.325 --> 43:13.362
and it seems to belong to a political group managed by your father.

770
43:13.429 --> 43:17.933
But he died ten years ago.

771
43:18.567 --> 43:20.002
Who is managing it?

772
43:20.069 --> 43:22.871
I'll ask Risuno Bank to trace it…

773
43:22.938 --> 43:25.307
No. Let's not cause a ruckus.

774
43:26.075 --> 43:28.210
-But… -Let's do this.

775
43:29.178 --> 43:31.780
I'll donate ten million to the Prime Minister myself.

776
43:33.115 --> 43:35.884
Then, we won't owe him anything.

777
43:42.224 --> 43:43.892
Inui.

778
43:45.527 --> 43:46.695
Who even are you?

779
43:50.599 --> 43:53.302
He should be dead already.

780
43:54.803 --> 43:56.105
I'm no one.

781
43:57.673 --> 43:58.741
Just a ghost.

782
44:02.778 --> 44:05.414
Next episode, a grudge from 25 years ago.

783
44:05.481 --> 44:08.484
Yayoi, Natsume, Inui, and Shiro.

784
44:08.550 --> 44:12.488
Their pasts weave the lost decades of Nipponia!

785
44:13.055 --> 44:16.692
Wait, what are you doing? Why are you all buddy-buddy?

786
44:16.759 --> 44:19.094
What about the thief? Catch her! Why?!

787
44:19.161 --> 44:20.195
What?

788
44:20.262 --> 44:23.499
You got a new Prime Minister?! Who?! Seriously?!

789
44:23.565 --> 44:26.168
Are you okay, Nipponia? Hang in there, Nipponia!

790
44:26.735 --> 44:28.137
That was fun.

791
44:28.203 --> 44:30.072
Subtitle translation by: Quang N. N.
